  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 19: WHAT IS CLOUD COMPUTING?
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="cloud-intro"
    eyebrow="CLOUD COMPUTING"
    title="What Is Cloud Computing?"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT 542CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          <strong>Cloud computing</strong> means renting computer power, storage space, and software over the internet from companies like Amazon (AWS), Microsoft (Azure), and Google (GCP), instead of buying physical computers yourself.
        </p>

        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 mb-1 uppercase">How It Changes Computing:</h4>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-900 space-y-1.5 pl-4 list-disc font-medium">
            <li><strong>Like Electricity or Water</strong>: You plug in and pay only for what you consume each month.</li>
            <li><strong>No Building Maintenance</strong>: You do not need to buy air conditioning, backup generators, or rent server rooms.</li>
            <li><strong>Instant Setup</strong>: You can create 10 new servers in 30 seconds using a web dashboard or code script.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Cloud Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Cloud Provider",
              definition: "A company (like AWS or Google) that owns datacenters and rents access to them."
            },
            {
              term: "On-Demand",
              definition: "Getting resources immediately whenever you need them without waiting weeks."
            },
            {
              term: "Pay-As-You-Go",
              definition: "Billing based strictly on the exact minutes and gigabytes you use."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Use the electricity utility analogy: nobody builds their own power plant to run a house; we plug into the electrical grid.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 20: THE 5 ESSENTIAL CLOUD TRAITS (NIST)
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="cloud-nist-traits"
    eyebrow="CLOUD COMPUTING"
    title="How Cloud Computing Works: The Five Essential Traits (NIST)"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT 542CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) defines five characteristics that a system must have to be called a true cloud:
        </p>

        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300">
          <ol class="text-xs text-slate-900 space-y-1.5 pl-4 list-decimal font-medium">
            <li><strong>On-Demand Self-Service</strong>: You can start servers yourself instantly without calling support.</li>
            <li><strong>Broad Network Access</strong>: You can access services over standard internet from phones, laptops, or tablets.</li>
            <li><strong>Resource Pooling</strong>: Hardware is shared securely among many customers to keep prices low.</li>
            <li><strong>Rapid Elasticity</strong>: Systems automatically add servers during traffic spikes and remove them when traffic drops.</li>
            <li><strong>Measured Service</strong>: You get a clear bill showing exactly how many seconds and bytes were used.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Elasticity",
              definition: "The ability to automatically stretch bigger during busy times and shrink smaller when quiet."
            },
            {
              term: "Resource Pooling",
              definition: "Sharing large physical server farms securely among multiple clients."
            },
            {
              term: "Self-Service",
              definition: "Creating and managing servers directly from a web page or script."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain that an ordinary office server with no automatic elasticity or self-service is not a true cloud system.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 21: THE THREE SERVICE MODELS (IAAS, PAAS, SAAS)
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="service-models-overview"
    eyebrow="CLOUD SERVICE MODELS"
    title="The Three Cloud Service Models: IaaS, PaaS & SaaS"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT 542CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="IaaS (Infrastructure)" badge="Raw Machines" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Renting blank virtual computers, hard drives, and network wires.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          You choose the operating system (like Ubuntu Linux) and install everything yourself.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 font-bold m-0">Examples: Amazon EC2, Google Compute Engine, DigitalOcean.</p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="PaaS (Platform)" badge="App Platforms" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Renting a managed environment ready to run your code.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          You upload your code (like Node.js or Python). The provider handles the servers, OS updates, and scaling.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 font-bold m-0">Examples: Heroku, AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Vercel.</p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="SaaS (Software)" badge="Ready Applications" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Using complete applications directly in your browser.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          You don't manage any servers or code. You just log in and use the finished software.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 font-bold m-0">Examples: Google Docs, Gmail, Microsoft 365, Canva.</p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Use the Pizza as a Service analogy: IaaS is buying raw dough and toppings, PaaS is pizza delivered, SaaS is dining at a restaurant.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

