    unit="BIT 542CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          <strong>Cloud bursting</strong> is a hybrid cloud technique where normal daily traffic runs on private company servers, but when traffic spikes unexpectedly, extra requests "burst" to rented public cloud servers.
        </p>

        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300 space-y-2">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 uppercase">How It Works:</h4>
          <ol class="text-xs text-slate-900 space-y-1.5 pl-4 list-decimal font-medium">
            <li><strong>Normal Day</strong>: 100% of traffic is handled by your own company servers at low fixed cost.</li>
            <li><strong>Traffic Surge (e.g. Festival Sale)</strong>: Server load passes 80% capacity.</li>
            <li><strong>Automatic Burst</strong>: The system instantly starts 50 virtual servers on public cloud to handle the overflow traffic.</li>
            <li><strong>Scale Down</strong>: When traffic returns to normal, the public cloud servers shut down to stop billing.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Cloud Bursting",
              definition: "Temporarily offloading traffic surges from private servers to public cloud instances."
            },
            {
              term: "Baseline Load",
              definition: "The normal everyday level of computing work an organization handles."
            },
            {
              term: "Peak Load",
              definition: "The maximum temporary spike in traffic during holidays or major events."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Use university exam results day as an example: normal days need 2 servers, result release day needs 50 servers for 4 hours.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

